87:## Twingate private access (optional)
88-
89:Twingate is not a public tunnel and does not replace the addresses above. It
90-is a zero-trust access service, so the Minecraft server stays private and only
91:authorized Twingate users can reach it. Public players should keep using
92-Minekube, LocalXpose, bore, Serveo, Pinggy or tunnl.
93-
94:The server can run a Twingate Connector alongside the public tunnel:
95-
96:1. In the Twingate Admin Console, add a Connector to the remote network and
97-   generate its **Access Token** and **Refresh Token**.
98-2. Add the two connector tokens as repository secrets
99:   `TWINGATE_ACCESS_TOKEN` and `TWINGATE_REFRESH_TOKEN`.
100-3. Optional: if your access token does not contain the tenant name in its
101-   `aud` claim, add the network slug as the repository variable or secret
102:   `TWINGATE_NETWORK`.
103:4. In the Twingate Admin Console, open **Settings > API**, generate an Admin
104-   API token with **Read & Write**, and add it as the repository secret
105:   `TWINGATE_API_TOKEN`. Connector access/refresh tokens cannot create
106-   Resources; the Admin API token is only used to ensure the `minecraft`
107-   Resource exists.
108:5. Assign users or groups to the `minecraft` Resource in the Twingate Admin
109-   Console after it is created.
110-
111-The connector starts automatically when both token secrets are present. Set
112:the repository variable `TWINGATE_ENABLED=false` to disable it, or `true` to
113-require both secrets. The default `auto` mode skips the connector when the
114-secrets are not configured.
115-
116:`scripts/twingate-resource.py` runs after the connector and idempotently
117:creates the `minecraft` Resource through the Twingate Admin API. The default
118-Resource points at `127.0.0.1:25565` and restricts TCP to port `25565` with
119-UDP and ICMP disabled. It is created in the only Remote Network in the
120-account; if there are several, set the repository variable
121:`TWINGATE_REMOTE_NETWORK` (name) or `TWINGATE_REMOTE_NETWORK_ID`. Resource
122-settings can be overridden with the repository variables
123:`TWINGATE_RESOURCE_NAME`, `TWINGATE_RESOURCE_ADDRESS` and
124:`TWINGATE_RESOURCE_PORT`. The script never prints token values. If no API
125:token is configured it skips cleanly; set `TWINGATE_RESOURCE_ENABLED=true` to
126-make a missing Admin API token fatal instead.
127-
128:The standalone **Ensure Twingate Resource** workflow can also be run manually
129-from the Actions tab. This is useful after adding or rotating
130:`TWINGATE_API_TOKEN` without starting the Minecraft server. For backward
131:compatibility the script falls back to `TWINGATE_ACCESS_TOKEN` when
132:`TWINGATE_API_TOKEN` is absent, but that only works if the access token is
133-actually an Admin API token rather than a Connector token.
134-
135:`scripts/twingate-connector.sh` runs the pinned
136:`twingate/connector:1` container with host networking and reads the connector
137:credentials only from the environment. It reads `TWINGATE_NETWORK` from the
138-access token's `aud` claim when no explicit network is supplied. It never
139-writes token values to the repository or logs. The connector is optional and
140-a connector failure does not stop the public tunnel.
141-
142-Host networking allows the connector to reach the Minecraft server at
143:`127.0.0.1:25565`, but Docker rejects the Twingate-recommended
144-`net.ipv4.ping_group_range` sysctl in the host network namespace. The script
145-therefore applies that sysctl only for non-host modes. Set
146:`TWINGATE_DOCKER_NETWORK=bridge` only if your resource is reachable through
147-the Docker bridge gateway instead of localhost.
148-
149:Do not paste Twingate tokens into issues, pull requests, chat or commits. A
150-token that has been posted publicly must be revoked and regenerated in the
151:Twingate Admin Console. The value pasted into issue 9 was not used or stored
152-by this repository.
